        <h2>The Real Timeline: Start to Finish</h2>
        <p>People often ask, "How long does the Non-Lucrative Visa take?" The answer depends on where in the process you're measuring from. If you're asking how long the consulate takes to process your application, it's typically 4–12 weeks. But if you're asking how long from deciding to apply to walking off the plane in Spain, budget 3–6 months.</p>
      </div>

      <p>The difference is crucial. Most of the time isn't spent waiting for the consulate to review your application. It's spent gathering documents, getting them certified, waiting for banking systems to produce statements, and scheduling your consulate appointment. The consulate processing is often the fastest part of the entire journey.</p>

      <p>This guide breaks down the timeline into realistic phases, so you understand what to expect and can plan your move accordingly.</p>

      <h2 style="margin-top: 3rem;">Phase 1: Document Preparation (4–8 weeks)</h2>
      <p>This is where most delays happen. Before you even contact the consulate, you need to gather and prepare extensive documentation.</p>

      <h3>Step 1: Assess Your Income (Week 1)</h3>
      <p>Determine how you'll prove your income. Most people use passive income: pensions, investments, rental income, or savings. Identify your specific income sources and how you'll document them. If your income comes from multiple sources, you'll need documentation from each.</p>

      <h3>Step 2: Request Documents from Financial Institutions (Week 1–2)</h3>
      <p>Contact your banks, investment firms, pension providers, and other financial institutions. Request: (1) Bank statements (12 months minimum), (2) Proof of funds on hand, (3) Dividend/interest statements, (4) Pension payment documentation, (5) Asset statements. Many institutions take 2–4 weeks to produce official statements. Start this immediately—it's the longest part of document gathering.</p>

      <p>Pro tip: Use online banking platforms to download statements directly; this is faster than requesting official documents. However, you'll also need official certified statements from the financial institution for the consulate.</p>

      <h3>Step 3: Get Documents Certified (Week 3–4)</h3>
      <p>Some documents need official certification or notarization. This varies by country and consulate. Common requirements: (1) Bank statements certified by the financial institution, (2) Income documents notarized, (3) Police clearance certificate (obtained from your local police), (4) Doctor's certificate of good health (some consulates), (5) Rental or property ownership documents certified. Start this early; notaries and government offices have backlogs.</p>

      <h3>Step 4: Obtain Police Clearance (Week 4–8)</h3>
      <p>Most countries have a background check process. In the US, it's typically through the FBI or local police. In the UK, through Disclosure and Barring Service (DBS). In Canada, through RCMP or local police. Australia has ASIC. This can take 4–8 weeks depending on your country. Request this early and don't wait for other documents to finish.</p>

      <h3>Step 5: Prepare Medical Clearance (if required) (Week 6–8)</h3>
      <p>Some consulates require proof of good health. Get a health certificate from your doctor stating you have no communicable diseases. Most GPs can provide this in 1–2 weeks. This isn't invasive—it's a basic health declaration.</p>

      <h3>Step 6: Organize and Translate Documents (Week 7–8)</h3>
      <p>Organize all documents in the order requested by the consulate. Many documents need translation into Spanish by official translators. Budget 2–3 weeks for professional translation of 10–20 pages. Some documents (government-issued) need apostille certification (an international official stamp). In the US, apostilles come from Secretary of State offices; in the UK, from the Foreign Office. This typically takes 1–3 weeks.</p>

      <div class="msv__alert msv__alert--warn">
        <span class="msv__alert-icon">⚠️</span>
        <p><strong>Critical timing issue:</strong> Don't wait for every document before starting this phase. Start requesting and certifying documents as soon as possible. Many can be done in parallel. The police clearance and bank statements are the longest—begin immediately.</p>
      </div>

      <h2 style="margin-top: 3rem;">Phase 2: Consulate Appointment and Application Submission (2–8 weeks)</h2>
      <p>Once your documents are ready, you need to schedule a consulate appointment and submit your application.</p>

      <h3>Step 1: Book Your Consulate Appointment (Week 1–4)</h3>
      <p>Contact your nearest Spanish consulate and request an appointment for the Non-Lucrative Visa. Most consulates have online appointment systems. During non-peak times (September–January), you can often get an appointment within 2–3 weeks. During peak season (April–August), it can take 6–8 weeks. Plan accordingly.</p>

      <p>Pro tip: Check if your consulate allows submitting applications by mail or email instead of in-person. Some do, which eliminates travel to the consulate and can speed things up.</p>

      <h3>Step 2: Attend Your Consulate Appointment (Week 5–8)</h3>
      <p>On your appointment date, arrive early with all your documents. The consulate will: (1) Review your documents for completeness, (2) Ask questions about your income and plans in Spain, (3) Potentially request additional documentation if anything is missing, (4) Take your photograph and fingerprints (for background check), (5) Provide a receipt with an estimated completion date. This appointment typically lasts 15–30 minutes.</p>

      <p>During this meeting, ask explicitly for an estimated completion date. Write it down. If the consulate says 6 weeks, plan for 8 weeks to be safe.</p>

      <h3>Step 3: Wait for Approval (Week 6–12 from submission)</h3>
      <p>This is the longest phase. The consulate processes your application behind the scenes: conducting background checks, verifying your income documentation, potentially requesting clarification from financial institutions. This typically takes 4–12 weeks. You'll receive notification (via email or mail) when your visa is approved.</p>

      <h2 style="margin-top: 3rem;">Processing Times by Consulate</h2>
      <p>Processing times vary significantly by location. These estimates are based on 2025–2026 data:</p>

