      <p>UK and US consulates tend to be relatively fast. Latin American consulates are slower due to higher application volume and slower administrative systems. Australian and Canadian consulates are moderate. Always contact your specific consulate for their current processing estimates—they may have updated information.</p>

      <h2 style="margin-top: 3rem;">Common Delays and How to Avoid Them</h2>
      <p>Understanding what causes delays helps you prevent them:</p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Incomplete documentation:</strong> Missing one document means automatic request for additional materials and 2–4 week delay. Avoid: Submit a detailed checklist with your application confirming every item is included.</li>
        <li><strong>Poor quality scans:</strong> Unreadable documents get rejected and require resubmission. Avoid: Scan documents at high quality (300 DPI minimum) and test readability before submitting.</li>
        <li><strong>Documents not certified:</strong> Some documents require official certification which you've forgotten. Avoid: Double-check with the consulate about which specific documents need certification before your appointment.</li>
        <li><strong>Income source unclear:</strong> If your income isn't obvious (e.g., you list "dividend income" but haven't explained which company), the consulate requests clarification. Avoid: Include a one-page summary explaining your income sources with supporting documents clearly labeled.</li>
        <li><strong>Background check issues:</strong> Criminal records, even minor ones, can trigger additional investigation. Avoid: If you have any record, disclose it upfront to the consulate; transparency often results in faster resolution.</li>
        <li><strong>Banking system delays:</strong> Your bank takes 6 weeks to produce official statements. Avoid: Request statements 3 months before your planned consulate appointment.</li>
        <li><strong>Consulate understaffing:</strong> Some consulates have staff shortages, especially during summer. Avoid: Apply during low-season months (September–January) when staff is fully allocated.</li>
        <li><strong>Unresponsiveness to consulate requests:</strong> If the consulate requests additional documents and you take 2 weeks to respond, it delays your case. Avoid: Check your email and mail daily during processing; respond to any consulate request within 3–5 days.</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="msv__alert msv__alert--green">
