        <p><strong>Pro tip:</strong> Use a professional visa specialist or gestor (immigration advisor) to prepare your application. They know which documents are most commonly missing and ensure everything is perfect first time. This typically cuts processing time by 4–6 weeks and eliminates delays from incomplete submissions.</p>
      </div>

      <h2 style="margin-top: 3rem;">Phase 3: Visa Issuance and Travel (1–2 weeks)</h2>
      <p>Once approved, the consulate produces your physical visa stamp and either mails it to you or has you pick it up in person. This typically takes 1–2 weeks. You then have 3 months from the date your visa is issued to enter Spain. Plan your flight accordingly.</p>

      <h2 style="margin-top: 3rem;">Phase 4: Arrival and Residency Registration in Spain (1–4 weeks)</h2>
      <p>Once you're in Spain, you still have administrative steps to complete:</p>

      <p><strong>NIE Registration (National ID Number):</strong> Within 3 months of arrival, you must register for a Spanish NIE (Número de Identidad de Extranjero) at your local police station (Policía Nacional). This is where you officially complete your residency. Processing takes 1–2 weeks. You'll receive your NIE number immediately but must wait for your residency card (TIE—Tarjeta de Identidad de Extranjero) which takes 2–4 weeks.</p>

      <p><strong>Empadronamiento (Local Registration):</strong> Register with your local municipal office (Ayuntamiento) to establish residency in your specific city/municipality. This is required to access healthcare, schools, and services. Processing typically takes 1 week. You need proof of address (rental contract or property deed).</p>

      <p><strong>Healthcare Registration:</strong> Once registered locally, enroll with the public healthcare system (Seguridad Social). This gives you access to Spanish healthcare. Takes 1–2 weeks.</p>

      <p>The total time from arrival in Spain to having your NIE and local residency officially registered is typically 2–4 weeks, depending on your local office's workload.</p>

      <h2 style="margin-top: 3rem;">How to Speed Up Your Application</h2>
      <p><strong>1. Prepare Aggressively</strong> Don't wait for your consulate appointment to start gathering documents. Begin 3–4 months before your target travel date. Request bank statements, police clearance, and medical certificates immediately.</p>

      <p><strong>2. Over-Document</strong> Include extra supporting documents. Yes, you need 12 months of bank statements, but include 24 months if possible. Yes, you need one proof of residence, but include multiple. Extra documentation shows seriousness and prevents requests for additional materials.</p>

      <p><strong>3. Use Professional Help</strong> A visa specialist or gestor prepares applications that are accepted first time. Yes, they cost €300–€1,000, but they eliminate delays and can save you 4–8 weeks. For most people, this pays for itself through faster processing.</p>

      <p><strong>4. Apply in Low Season</strong> September–January is the slowest period for visa applications. Fewer people apply, so consulates process faster. If you apply in May or June, expect 2–4 week delays compared to applying in October.</p>

      <p><strong>5. Respond Immediately to Consulate Requests</strong> If the consulate requests additional documentation, respond within 3–5 days. Delays in your response delay your entire case.</p>

      <p><strong>6. Consider Expedited Processing</strong> Some consulates offer expedited processing for an additional fee (typically €50–€150). Not all do, but ask yours. It can cut processing time by 30%.</p>
