            <p>Common delay causes: (1) Incomplete or unclear income documentation, (2) Poor quality document scans, (3) Documents not certified by official bodies, (4) Income source not clearly explained, (5) Background check complications, (6) Police clearance delays, (7) Banking system holidays, (8) Consulate understaffing during peak season, (9) Request for additional documentation from the consulate, (10) Your unresponsiveness to consulate queries.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>Can you speed up the Non-Lucrative Visa process?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>To accelerate: (1) Submit a complete, high-quality application first time, (2) Include extra supporting documents (bank statements from 12 months), (3) Respond immediately to any consulate requests, (4) Apply in low-season months (September–January), (5) Use a visa specialist or gestor to prepare documents professionally, (6) Some consulates offer expedited processing for an additional fee (check yours). Professional help often cuts processing time by 4–6 weeks.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>What should you do while waiting for your Non-Lucrative Visa?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>During the waiting period: (1) Finalize your move logistics (housing, shipping, etc.), (2) Research neighborhoods and cities in Spain, (3) Book NIE (ID number) appointment slot in Spain (via online system), (4) Open a Spanish bank account online (some banks allow non-residents), (5) Learn Spanish basics via apps or courses, (6) Research healthcare options and insurance, (7) Connect with expat communities for insights, (8) Organize accommodation for first 1–2 months in Spain.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>What happens after your Non-Lucrative Visa is approved?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>After approval: (1) You receive your visa-stamped passport (typically arrives 1–2 weeks after approval), (2) You have 3 months to enter Spain from visa date, (3) You must report to the local Policía Nacional within 3 months to complete procedures, (4) You book an appointment with the National Police to complete your residency registration, (5) You receive your TIE (residency card) in 2–4 weeks, (6) Your visa is valid for 1 year; you then apply for residency renewal.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
